   200	
   201	The smoke stack brings up three agents and their recorders, answers them from a
   202	stub model that replies on cue instead of a real one, and mounts the contract
   203	fixture as the window, on scratch directories with a dummy key and its own
   204	compose project. It costs nothing. A second stack runs the same fleet with the
   205	vehicle as the window (`live/test_vehicle.py`); it replaces the first, and a
   206	stack refuses to start while another smoke project is running. Every container
   207	is capped (`CAPS` in `live/stack.py`): CPUs, and memory with no swap past it,
   208	read back from each container's cgroup before any test runs. A stack's caps sum
   209	to 4.3 CPUs and 12 GiB, inside the live run's budget of 5 CPUs and 14 GiB
   210	(Governance, 2026-10-11). The images are built one at a time. Run it inside a
   211	user scope, which caps pytest and the docker clients:
   212	
   213	```sh
   214	systemd-run --user --scope -p CPUQuota=50% -p MemoryMax=1G nice -n 19 python3 -m pytest live -q
   215	```
   216	
   217	Two things no client can cap: the docker daemon's own work for the stack, and
   218	the builds. Docker's default builder runs every build step inside the daemon,
   219	and neither compose nor `docker build` offers a CPU or memory limit for it.
   220	
   221	It builds the image, runs the containment and acceptance checks against the
   222	running stack — no route out, the binds, the caps, a `done`, a reseed — and
   223	tears it down. It takes a few minutes, and it is the fastest way to see whether
   224	the world is intact after a change.
   225	
   226	## The vehicle
   227	
   228	The `vehicle` profile runs the spacecraft: one executive for every agent's
   229	window, with no network, its private state (checkpoints, its lock and the
   230	durable per-cycle record) on a `/state` image nothing else mounts. The windows
   215	CHECKPOINT_EVERY = 50
   216	# A result ends in its receipt line; `tick` is the tick its cycle began at (the receipt is drawn
   217	# before the step), and that cycle's record row is the tick after it.
   218	RECEIPT = re.compile(r"^receipt: world=(\S+) seq=\d+ window=\S+ tick=(\d+) ", re.MULTILINE)
   219	RESUMED = re.compile(
